 To create a Java method that takes a List<Car> and returns the count and color of the most popular Car.getColor, we can use the Java Stream API for a concise and efficient solution. Here's an example of the method:

```java
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class Car {
    private String color;

    // Constructor, getters, and setters...

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Car[color=" + color + "]";
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Car> cars = Arrays.asList(
                new Car("Red"),
                new Car("Blue"),
                new Car("Red"),
                new Car("Green"),
                new Car("Blue"),
                new Car("Red"),
                new Car("Green")
        );

        Map<String, Long> colorCountMap = countAndGroupByColor(cars);
        String mostPopularColor = findMostPopularColor(colorCountMap);
        long mostPopularCount = colorCountMap.get(mostPopularColor);

        System.out.println("The most popular color is " + mostPopularColor + " with " + mostPopularCount + " cars.");
    }

    public static Map<String, Long> countAndGroupByColor(List<Car> cars) {
        return cars.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Car::getColor, Collectors.counting()));
    }

    public static String findMostPopularColor(Map<String, Long> colorCountMap) {
        return Collections.max(colorCountMap.entrySet(), Map.Entry.comparingByValue()).getKey();
    }
}
```

This example demonstrates a Java method that takes a List<Car> and returns the count and color of the most popular Car.getColor by first grouping the cars by their color and then counting the occurrences of each color. The method `findMostPopularColor` finds the color with the maximum count from the grouped results.

