```

This code works by splitting the string into an array of characters, reversing the order of the array, and then joining the elements back into a single string. If you want the reversed string in the correct order, you can call the `reverse()` method again on the string after joining the characters back together:

```javascript
let myString = "Hello, World!";
let reversedString = myString.split("").reverse().join("").reverse();
console.log(reversedString); // Output: "!dlrow olleH"
```

Now the reversed string is in the correct order.

